    "source_file_to_entries": {
        "raw-wiktextract-data.jsonl": [
            {
                "entry_id": "db3a3ed4-5af1-3073-b3b0-fda8a121602b",
                "word": "apostrophus",
                "pos": "noun",
                "lang_code": "en",
                "forms": [
                    {
                        "form_id": "ee1cdc81-4753-4d70-bd11-03b1e846668d",
                        "entry_id": "db3a3ed4-5af1-3073-b3b0-fda8a121602b",
                        "tags": [
                            "plural"
                        ],
                        "form": "apostrophi"
                    }
                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "07e24045-7d7d-3a42-a69c-73d5dd1de365",
                        "entry_id": "db3a3ed4-5af1-3073-b3b0-fda8a121602b",
                        "glosses": [
                            "The omission of a vowel or syllable from a word, usually indicated by the apostrophe symbol ( ' ). examples: 'til, can't"
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "archaic"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "ebc908bf-8954-446c-a90f-71264cdc334e",
                                "sense_id": "07e24045-7d7d-3a42-a69c-73d5dd1de365",
                                "text": "Vowels suffer also this apostrophus before the consonant h."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "e7e3162a-625f-470b-87dd-91dc40a9fe7c",
                                "sense_id": "07e24045-7d7d-3a42-a69c-73d5dd1de365",
                                "text": "Not that any examples are wanting to prove that the ancients did use the apostrophus in prose; for it was scarecely possible for them not to do so in many instances;"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "98a1a149-38b7-46cf-8645-5084e2e8c5e4",
                                "sense_id": "07e24045-7d7d-3a42-a69c-73d5dd1de365",
                                "text": "Out of one word, the apostrophus is most usual in poesie;"
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "58d64f99-93e4-3966-88f3-56bab82bdbfa",
                        "entry_id": "db3a3ed4-5af1-3073-b3b0-fda8a121602b",
                        "glosses": [
                            "The apostrophe symbol used to mark this elision."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "archaic"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "da74181c-fde2-47d5-9299-bff9dcdc5451",
                                "sense_id": "58d64f99-93e4-3966-88f3-56bab82bdbfa",
                                "text": "The apostrophus or mark of contraction, is the same with the comma, onely the difference is of place; for, this stands not in the line, but over the upper part thereof, where the contraction is:"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "bdc26322-849e-45ae-98a0-19e9f8da3abb",
                                "sense_id": "58d64f99-93e4-3966-88f3-56bab82bdbfa",
                                "text": "In the Greek, as in other languages, a short vowel at the end of a word before another vowel, is thrown out by elision, and the apostrophus ' is placed as a mark or sign over the empty space;"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "1c1ab224-6707-4df1-9c50-074b8a8a2820",
                                "sense_id": "58d64f99-93e4-3966-88f3-56bab82bdbfa",
                                "text": "This apocope is denoted by the apostrophus ( ' ),"
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "f503359d-c719-3563-b84e-d2c0dc7a1942",
                        "entry_id": "db3a3ed4-5af1-3073-b3b0-fda8a121602b",
                        "glosses": [
                            "An open notehead ( ~ ) or an apostrophe ( ' ) used as a neume to mark a low, unaccented syllable at the beginning of a phrase or a short quick syllable"
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "historical"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "31ee51da-d48e-495b-9aa6-1c22c7d43a5c",
                                "sense_id": "f503359d-c719-3563-b84e-d2c0dc7a1942",
                                "text": "You find not the apostrophus, and so miss the accent. Let me supervise the canzonet."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "b1e4799b-836b-45a8-9a42-8a7fe1dff843",
                                "sense_id": "f503359d-c719-3563-b84e-d2c0dc7a1942",
                                "text": "In the early Graduals Benevento VI. 38 and 40, the apostrophus is common in syllabic passages in sequences, tropes and prosulae."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "5907f147-194a-4380-8cfe-6fd2e151e6d9",
                                "sense_id": "f503359d-c719-3563-b84e-d2c0dc7a1942",
                                "text": "In transcriptions from Ben 34, I have not distinguished the apostrophus from regular notes."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "e1cf6520-d303-4ac5-ba4d-438f3d97bb99",
                                "sense_id": "f503359d-c719-3563-b84e-d2c0dc7a1942",
                                "text": "The early Beneventan apostrophus was optional, instructive and exemplary, suggesting low, quick, unaccented syllables at the start of a phrase (sometimes implying a preceding breath) in the Gregorian Mass Propers and short or quick syllables in tropes, prosulae and sequences."
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "bbbcce2d-52b1-3155-8e89-37b368be567e",
                        "entry_id": "db3a3ed4-5af1-3073-b3b0-fda8a121602b",
                        "glosses": [
                            "The symbol 'Ↄ', used in Roman numerals to indicate multiplication by 10."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "e5391e98-d69e-4ddc-8b0d-2adc6b78bcb5",
                                "sense_id": "bbbcce2d-52b1-3155-8e89-37b368be567e",
                                "text": "In every multiplication with ten a fresh apostrophus is added; thus IↃↃ = 5000, IↃↃↃ = 50,000."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "9b90b2c1-85ad-47f4-8253-c585592ae2a3",
                                "sense_id": "bbbcce2d-52b1-3155-8e89-37b368be567e",
                                "text": "The annexing of the apostrophus or inverted C (Ↄ) to IↃ makes its value ten times greater;"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "ad39fe6b-f831-4a70-8645-5c68b4d845f8",
                                "sense_id": "bbbcce2d-52b1-3155-8e89-37b368be567e",
                                "text": "The symbol Ↄ, known as the apostrophus, might be repeated one or more times after IↃ , each Ↄ making the number ten times greater, as IↃↃ = 5000, IↃↃↃↃ = 500,000."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "e4668dec-a60f-4521-a7ca-98bcebe42b64",
                                "sense_id": "bbbcce2d-52b1-3155-8e89-37b368be567e",
                                "text": "Anno CIↃ IↃ C LXXXVI. (Apostrophus numbering): In the year 1686"
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
